 "caption": "Figure F5. Every method we tested collapses on the omission whose trace survives elsewhere in the note, and the supply of those cases says why. (a) Paired discrimination on omissions - how often a note carrying an omission scores below its own verified-clean twin, tie-adjusted, where 0.5 is a coin flip - split by how much of the removed fact survives: nothing (complete removal), a fragment of it (fragment trace), or an explicit or closely paraphrased second mention (restatement trace). Each line names its own substrate, and the two substrates are read separately rather than ranked against each other: the best of the eight judge designs, the range across the eight reference judges, the naive RAGAS-style coverage recipe (our implementation, read as its coverage score rather than at its own flag-everything rule), and the budgeted single call - route two, the optimiser's winner at reasoning effort high, three replicates averaged - are measured on the 495-pair evaluation set, whose 293 omission pairs split 150 / 86 / 57 across the three levels; the two-stage and three-stage pipelines are measured on the 151-pair held-out subset, whose 131 omission pairs split 62 / 36 / 33. The coverage recipe is the one line with denominators of its own, 148 / 84 / 57, because parse failures cost it 14 of its 1,214 records. The interval printed beside each restatement-trace value is a consultation-clustered bootstrap 95% interval, computed over 35 consultations for the evaluation-set line and 20 for the held-out ones, and every one of them contains 0.5; the coverage recipe's and the budgeted call's restatement-trace values have no such interval in the released artifacts and are quoted as point estimates. Detection collapses with discrimination: the per-fact critical rule flags 0 of 33 held-out restatement-trace notes against 1 of 47 clean twins, 0 of 57 on the full evaluation set against 3 of 112, and the budgeted call's own rule reads 4 of 57 there against its 6.2% false-alarm rate - at or below each rule's own noise. (b) Where the surviving mention sits, over all 148 partial-omission pairs in the corpus (61 restatement-trace, of which 57 are in the evaluation set, and 87 fragment-trace, of which 86 are): in 55 of 61 restatement-trace pairs, 90.2% [80.2, 95.4], the survivor sits under a different heading from the instance that was removed, while 49 of 87 fragment-trace pairs, 56.3% [45.9, 66.3], keep it under the same heading; two of the six same-heading restatement traces are notes with no headings at all. Reading: once a restatement trace survives, every method sits at or below its own noise floor, and the supply explains why - a restatement trace is almost always a different-heading survivor, recoverable only by reading the note end to end.",
 "conventions": {
  "paired": "tie-adjusted probability that the errored note scores strictly below its own clean twin, plus half the tie mass; chance is 0.500",
  "substrates": {
   "evaluation set": "495 pairs, 293 omissions, 112 clean twins",
   "held out": "151 pairs, 131 omissions, 47 clean twins"
  },
  "convention_note": "FINDINGS 18.4 (line 607) quotes the coverage recipe's residual split as 0.915 / 0.829 / 0.518 on the mean-of-replicates convention; the pooled convention that 18.4 declares for the arm-level figure, and that _recomputed.json carries, gives 0.9206 / 0.8363 / 0.5175. This figure plots the pooled values. The restatement-trace value agrees to three decimals."
 },
